# t1-lecture.tr (Level 1): the Lecture 11 fragmentation example.
# The handout walks through it request by request.


# Build the heap from the Lecture 11 slide "Fragmentation":
# four blocks of 32, 64, 32 and 96 bytes (requests of 16, 48, 16, 80).
a 0 16
a 1 48
a 2 16
a 3 80

# 8 bytes of fence + 32 + 64 + 32 + 96 = 232.
h 232

# Free the 64- and 96-byte blocks: [Alloc 32][Free 64][Alloc 32][Free 96].
f 1
f 3
a 4 112

# A 112-byte request needs a 128-byte block. 160 bytes are free, but in
# two pieces of 64 and 96, so no free block fits: the slide's external
# fragmentation. The heap grows by one 128-byte block: 232 + 128 = 360.
h 360
a 5 40

# A 40-byte request needs a 64-byte block. First fit finds the free
# 64-byte block at heap+40 and reuses it, so the heap must not grow.
h 360
